    "result_text": "The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented country-specific national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `TelephoneNormalizer.java`.\n\n**Changes** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/TelephoneNormalizer.java`):\n\n1. Added a `NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE` map (ordered longest-prefix-first via `LinkedHashMap`) declaring the required national-digit counts: `+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10 national digits.\n\n2. Added a `validateNationalNumberLength(...)` method that, after the existing generic 8\u201315 digit bounds check, strips the country code from the normalized E.164 value and rejects the number with an `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 HTTP 400) when its national-number length doesn't match the recognized country's requirement. Unknown country codes fall back to the generic bounds (no new restriction).\n\n3. Wired the check into `normalize(...)` so it runs on every create-owner request, while `canonicalize(...)` (used only for duplicate detection of already-stored numbers) is deliberately left untouched.\n\nThe mutually-exclusive prefixes (`61` vs `1`) are matched longest-first so the correct country code always wins. The valid baseline telephone (a 10-digit AU mobile \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still passes, and the new acceptance test `errorRejectsWrongNationalLength` (`+61 123`) is rejected with 400.\n\n`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
